{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    // WHY EVERY RELATIVE IMPORT IN src/ ENDS IN .js
    //
    // This package is "type": "module", so Node resolves the COMPILED output
    // with ESM rules, which demand a real file extension on every relative
    // specifier. TypeScript emits specifiers verbatim, so an extensionless
    // `./lib/prisma` compiles cleanly and then dies at runtime with
    // ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND. tsx resolves like a bundler, so dev never sees it.
    //
    // "Bundler" ALLOWS the .js suffix (mapping ./x.js -> x.ts); it just does
    // not require it. NodeNext would require it and catch a future omission at
    // compile time, which is strictly better — but Vercel's builder type-checks
    // this project with its own TypeScript setup and reports errors under
    // NodeNext that our own tsc 5.9.3 does not (openai "not constructable",
    // unresolved generated Prisma client). Bundler is the resolution mode that
    // platform demonstrably compiles, so it stays.
    //
    // The tradeoff: nothing here fails the build if someone adds an import
    // without .js. `node dist/index.js` is what catches that — see README.
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "strict": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "outDir": "dist",
    "rootDir": "src"
  },
  "include": ["src"]
}